      <div class="chr-prose" id="chr-body">
        <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
        <p>Most of us know the feeling. You walk through the front door after a long day, see the pile of post on the side, the washing up in the sink and the shoes across the hall, and something in you sinks a little. Or the opposite: you come home to a fresh, tidy room, and your shoulders drop.</p>
        <p>Is there evidence behind these feelings? Psychologists, neuroscientists and sleep researchers have begun to look at the link between the state of our homes and the state of our minds. The research is young, and mostly shows associations rather than cause and effect. But a consistent picture is emerging: our living spaces are linked with how stressed we feel, how well we sleep, how easily we focus and how we get on with the people we live with.</p>
        <p>It is a question that matters in the UK. The Office for National Statistics (ONS) tracks personal wellbeing every quarter. In April to June 2026, the average UK adult rated their anxiety "yesterday" at 3.2 out of 10, and 22.5% gave a high anxiety score of 6 or more (<a href="https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/wellbeing/datasets/quarterlypersonalwellbeingestimatesseasonallyadjusted" rel="noopener">ONS, 2026</a>). The ONS does not ask about homes, so these figures show the backdrop, not a link with clutter.</p>
        <aside class="chr-note">
          <p class="chr-kicker">Backdrop, not a finding about homes</p>
          <p>ONS, April to June 2026: the average UK adult rated anxiety "yesterday" at 3.2 out of 10, and 22.5% gave a high anxiety score of 6 or more. The ONS does not ask about homes.</p>
        </aside>
        <p>This report brings together what published studies actually say, and what each one can and cannot tell us. The aim is not to make anyone feel guilty about a messy kitchen. Real homes get messy. The aim is to understand why a cared for home can feel so good, and which small, realistic habits might help.</p>

        <h2 id="mood-and-stress">Mood and stress</h2>
        <h3>What a home "says" about our stress</h3>
        <p>One of the most cited studies here comes from UCLA. Psychologists Darby Saxbe and Rena Repetti studied 60 working parents from 30 dual income families (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/0146167209352864" rel="noopener">Saxbe &amp; Repetti, 2010</a>). Each parent gave a self guided video tour of their home, and software counted words describing clutter or unfinished projects, and restful or nature words.</p>
        <p>Over the next three weekdays, parents gave saliva samples to track cortisol, a stress related hormone that normally starts high in the morning and falls through the day. Wives whose home tours scored higher on the "stressful home" measure (clutter and unfinished project words) had flatter cortisol slopes across the day, a profile the authors note is associated with adverse health outcomes. They also showed increasing depressed mood over the course of the day. Wives with higher "restorative home" scores showed the opposite pattern. These links held after accounting for marital satisfaction and neuroticism.</p>
        <p>This study measured how people <em>talked about</em> their homes, not how messy they actually were, and the reported findings concern the wives. It is small and shows a link, not proof that clutter raises stress.</p>

        <h3>Clutter and wellbeing</h3>
        <p>Catherine Roster, Joseph Ferrari and M. Peter Jurkat took a broader approach (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvp.2016.03.003" rel="noopener">Roster, Ferrari &amp; Jurkat, 2016</a>). They defined clutter as "an overabundance of possessions that collectively create chaotic and disorderly living spaces" and surveyed adults in the US and Canada. According to <a href="https://www.newswise.com/articles/cutting-through-the-clutter-study-examines-dark-side-of-home" rel="noopener">DePaul University</a>, the study received just under 1,400 usable responses, mostly from women, recruited through a non profit organisation that supports people who struggle with chronic disorganisation.</p>
        <p>Attachment to home and to possessions supported a sense of "psychological home", the feeling that your home reflects and belongs to you. Clutter worked against this: it was associated with a weaker sense of psychological home and lower subjective wellbeing. Because participants already had some difficulty with clutter, the results may not apply equally to everyone.</p>

        <h3>It's how you feel, not how it looks</h3>
        <p>A UK led study adds an important nuance. Caroline Rogers and Rona Hart, then at the University of East London (Hart is now at the University of Sussex), surveyed 1,111 adults, most of them women (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvp.2021.101553" rel="noopener">Rogers &amp; Hart, 2021</a>). They measured how much clutter people had, how they felt about it, how they expressed themselves through their homes and how often they decluttered, and compared these with an established wellbeing measure.</p>
        <p>Subjective clutter and psychological home accounted for a substantial share of the differences in wellbeing between people. Objective clutter, the actual amount of stuff, did not predict wellbeing. The authors proposed a new definition: clutter is "a subjective experience of possessions (material or other) that inhibits the curation of self-identity at home."</p>
        <p>This is reassuring: there is no single "correct" level of tidiness. A home full of books and photos can feel wonderful to one person and overwhelming to another. What seems to matter is whether your space feels manageable and feels like <em>yours</em>.</p>

        <h3>A calming chore?</h3>
        <p>Many Britons already find tidying rewarding. In YouGov polling from October 2025, 46% said they enjoy decluttering or clearing out their house and 40% said they like tidying up, compared with roughly a quarter who dislike each (<a href="https://yougov.com/en-gb/articles/53151-which-chores-do-britons-enjoy-doing" rel="noopener">YouGov, 2025</a>).</p>

