        <h2 id="sleep">Sleep</h2>
        <h3>The bedroom as a place to rest</h3>
        <p>Sleep advice usually stresses a cool, dark, quiet bedroom. Tidiness has been studied less, and the clearest research comes from hoarding, where clutter is extreme. At St Lawrence University in New York, psychologist Pamela Thacher and student researcher Alexis Reinheimer surveyed people recruited online who were interested in hoarding, sleep or clutter (<a href="https://aasm.org/people-at-risk-of-hoarding-disorder-may-have-serious-complaints-about-sleep/" rel="noopener">American Academy of Sleep Medicine, 2015</a>). Using a standard clutter and hoarding rating scale, they identified 83 people at risk of hoarding disorder and 198 at minimal or no risk (<a href="https://northcountrynow.com/stories/hoarders-dont-sleep-well-st-lawrence-university-researcher-finds,59013" rel="noopener">St Lawrence University coverage</a>). Those at risk scored significantly higher on a sleep habits survey and on three parts of a widely used sleep quality index: how long it took them to fall asleep, sleep disturbances and daytime disturbances. This was a conference abstract (SLEEP 2015), not a full journal article.</p>
        <p>A separate peer reviewed study from Florida State University looked at 24 patients with hoarding disorder and found that insomnia was a significant predictor of greater hoarding severity, with sleep difficulties associated with more acquiring and more difficulty discarding (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/jclp.22161" rel="noopener">Raines et al., 2015</a>). The sample was tiny and the direction of the link is unclear. Hoarding disorder is a recognised mental health condition, very different from an ordinary untidy bedroom, so these findings should not be stretched to everyday mess. Mind's guidance on hoarding suggests small, manageable steps, such as setting a timer to tidy one area, and points people to UK charities including Hoarding UK (<a href="https://www.mind.org.uk/information-support/types-of-mental-health-problems/hoarding/helping-yourself-with-hoarding/" rel="noopener">Mind</a>).</p>

        <h3>Clean sheets and a made bed</h3>
        <p>Poor sleep is common in the UK. In a 2025 Opinium survey of 2,000 UK adults for the Mental Health Foundation, the average adult reported only three days a week of good quality sleep, and 38% said poor sleep negatively affects their mental health at least once a week (<a href="https://www.mentalhealth.org.uk/about-us/news/average-adult-has-only-3-days-week-good-quality-sleep" rel="noopener">Mental Health Foundation, 2025</a>). The mental health charity Mind suggests trying different temperature, light and noise levels and different bedding to make your sleeping area more comfortable (<a href="https://www.mind.org.uk/information-support/types-of-mental-health-problems/sleep-problems/tips-to-improve-your-sleep/" rel="noopener">Mind</a>).</p>
        <aside class="chr-note">
          <p class="chr-kicker">UK sleep, not a clutter finding</p>
          <p>Mental Health Foundation / Opinium, 2025, 2,000 UK adults: the average adult reported only three days a week of good quality sleep, and 38% said poor sleep negatively affects their mental health at least once a week.</p>
        </aside>
        <p>For everyday homes, the best available evidence comes from surveys. The National Sleep Foundation's first Bedroom Poll interviewed 1,500 Americans aged 25 to 55 by telephone (<a href="https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/americans-bedrooms-are-key-to-better-sleep-according-to-new-national-sleep-foundation-poll-114529349.html" rel="noopener">National Sleep Foundation, 2011</a>). At least two thirds said that a clean bedroom, clean air free of allergens, a cool temperature, darkness and quiet play an important role in getting good sleep. Seventy eight per cent said they were more excited to go to bed on sheets with a fresh scent, and 71% said they got a more comfortable night's sleep on them. People who said they made their bed every day were 19% more likely to report a good night's sleep every night.</p>
        <p>These are self reports, not measured sleep, and the poll received financial support from a laundry brand (the foundation says it was developed independently). Nor can it tell us whether bed making improves sleep or good sleepers simply make their beds. Still, many people clearly <em>feel</em> a fresh, tidy bedroom helps them rest.</p>

