        <p>The UK picture is similar. In the ONS's latest time use survey (14 to 22 March 2026), women spent an average of about 3 hours 36 minutes a day on unpaid work, around 50 minutes more than men (about 2 hours 46 minutes). That included about 2 hours 43 minutes a day of unpaid household work, such as cooking and cleaning, compared with about 2 hours for men (<a href="https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/personalandhouseholdfinances/incomeandwealth/datasets/timeuseintheuk" rel="noopener">ONS, Time use in the UK, March 2026</a>; official statistics in development). An earlier, more detailed ONS analysis of 2015 data found that women did an average of 60% more unpaid work than men, including about 4.7 hours of housework a week compared with about 2.4 hours for men (<a href="https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/earningsandworkinghours/articles/womenshouldertheresponsibilityofunpaidwork/2016-11-10" rel="noopener">ONS, 2016</a>).</p>

